WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.888 --> 00:00:13.154
Hola, amigos!

2
00:00:14.215 --> 00:00:16.376
That's Spanish for "Hello, friends!"

3
00:00:17.497 --> 00:00:24.562
I'm learning Spanish because—get this—my parents might let me study abroad in Spain this summer!

4
00:00:25.742 --> 00:00:27.183
And it's all thanks to Mr.

5
00:00:28.648 --> 00:00:33.772
Fay. He told us about a landmark there that sounds so cool, I want to check it out.

6
00:00:34.753 --> 00:00:39.597
It's a medieval fortress and complex of palaces called the Alhambra.

7
00:00:40.658 --> 00:00:44.161
The word alhambra means "the red" in Arabic.

8
00:00:45.242 --> 00:00:51.707
The complex was named that because the entire place is surrounded by walls made of red gravel and

9
00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:52.488
clay bricks.

10
00:00:53.629 --> 00:01:01.626
From the outside the Alhambra looks pretty plain, but on the inside, everything is decorated with fancy tiles

11
00:01:01.686 --> 00:01:02.447
and carvings.

12
00:01:03.628 --> 00:01:11.472
Oh, and here's a cool fact: the walls are covered with calligraphy because the original builders were actually

13
00:01:11.672 --> 00:01:13.713
ordered to write all over them!

14
00:01:15.494 --> 00:01:18.836
So why does a place in Spain have an Arabic name?

15
00:01:19.476 --> 00:01:24.379
Mr. Fay had to give us a little history first so we could understand that.

16
00:01:25.379 --> 00:01:31.448
In the 700s Arab Muslims invaded and took over large parts of Spain.

17
00:01:32.529 --> 00:01:40.036
For the next 700 years or so, Spain was a battleground for Christians and these Muslims, known as

18
00:01:40.136 --> 00:01:49.284
Moors. Eventually, in 1492, Catholic monarchs regained control and ousted the Moors from their kingdom.

19
00:01:50.332 --> 00:01:57.296
But the Moors left their mark with the Alhambra, a beautiful walled city they'd built in Granada.

20
00:01:59.097 --> 00:02:06.341
Years before, when the Moors were fighting Christian armies, the rulers had needed a military fortress that would

21
00:02:06.381 --> 00:02:07.702
be naturally protected.

22
00:02:08.882 --> 00:02:14.465
On a rocky hill in southern Spain, they found an old fort from the eighth century.

23
00:02:15.546 --> 00:02:20.977
The hill was near a river, so there was access to water, and the area was protected by

24
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:22.218
mountains and woods.

25
00:02:23.318 --> 00:02:28.061
It was the ideal place to put their lookout tower and royal residence.

26
00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:37.687
Over time Moorish rulers continued to expand the Alhambra; construction began during the ninth century and continued through

27
00:02:37.707 --> 00:02:39.448
the late fifteenth century.

28
00:02:40.608 --> 00:02:49.378
At its peak the complex contained a military fortress, two palaces and courts, artisan workshops, baths, and a

29
00:02:49.478 --> 00:02:57.664
mosque. The oldest remaining section of the complex is the military area, known as the Alcazaba.

30
00:02:58.725 --> 00:03:04.969
It contains several towers, including the original watchtower, which is about 27 meters tall.

31
00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:13.075
After climbing the steep, narrow staircase to the top, royal guards could watch for invaders and even attack

32
00:03:13.235 --> 00:03:14.356
enemies from above.

33
00:03:15.532 --> 00:03:18.194
I bet it felt like the top of the world up there!

34
00:03:19.154 --> 00:03:27.258
The Alcazaba also had military barracks, secret passageways, and even underground dungeons for prisoners!

35
00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:33.142
Another section of the Alhambra is a great hall known as the Mexuar.

36
00:03:34.282 --> 00:03:37.484
Remember how I said there was lots of writing on the walls?

37
00:03:38.644 --> 00:03:46.406
Well, Islamic law forbids the drawing or painting of living things, so all the decorations in the Alhambra

38
00:03:46.706 --> 00:03:49.787
had to be geometric designs or calligraphy.

39
00:03:51.648 --> 00:03:55.229
I think it was a really smart idea to write on the walls.

40
00:03:56.389 --> 00:04:01.950
For example, at the door to the Mexuar, it says, "Enter and ask.

41
00:04:03.091 --> 00:04:07.372
Do not be afraid to seek justice for here you will find it."

42
00:04:08.572 --> 00:04:10.193
And guess what went on there?

43
00:04:11.174 --> 00:04:17.498
That's right—official government business, including all matters of justice and the law.

44
00:04:18.659 --> 00:04:25.043
It was where the sultan and government officials held meetings and public forums for people to voice complaints.

45
00:04:26.204 --> 00:04:30.127
The fanciest parts of the Alhambra are the palaces, of course.

46
00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:36.691
Two were built during the Arab rule, and a third was commissioned later by a Christian king in

47
00:04:36.711 --> 00:04:37.071
the 1500s.

48
00:04:38.876 --> 00:04:43.760
The two Arab palaces are the Comares Palace and the Palace of the Lions.

49
00:04:45.202 --> 00:04:52.408
The Comares Palace was the official royal residence, built between 1333 and 1370.

50
00:04:53.428 --> 00:04:56.311
The sultan lived there with his wives and guards.

51
00:04:57.272 --> 00:04:58.573
From the pictures Mr.

52
00:04:58.633 --> 00:05:02.056
Fay showed us, it sure looks like a sultan's home!

53
00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:08.860
The palace is centered around a courtyard that has a long pool fed by two fountains.

54
00:05:10.041 --> 00:05:15.625
Green myrtle bushes line the pool, giving the courtyard its name—the Court of Myrtle.

55
00:05:16.706 --> 00:05:23.471
In the right light, the water reflects the bushes' color, making the edges of the pool look green.

56
00:05:24.752 --> 00:05:30.656
The courtyard also has a white marble patio with intricately decorated arches and columns.

57
00:05:31.796 --> 00:05:37.959
Historians think the Alhambra was built primarily to show the greatness of Allah, or God.

58
00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:45.202
Every sight within the complex was supposed to remind people of the beauty of Allah's creation.

59
00:05:46.902 --> 00:05:54.425
The Palace of the Lions, another royal residence, was built between 1362 and 1391.

60
00:05:55.486 --> 00:06:03.754
Its courtyard has a magnificent fountain supported by twelve lions spouting water—the only representations of living forms in

61
00:06:03.794 --> 00:06:04.515
the Alhambra.

62
00:06:05.555 --> 00:06:13.401
The courtyard is supported by white marble columns, and its ceilings are decorated with carved, lace-like designs, and

63
00:06:13.521 --> 00:06:14.602
fancy calligraphy.

64
00:06:15.783 --> 00:06:21.507
I can't even imagine how long it must have taken to carve all that by hand!

65
00:06:21.727 --> 00:06:30.013
In 1527 the Christian king, Charles V, decided that he liked the Alhambra so much, he wanted to

66
00:06:30.053 --> 00:06:32.215
build himself a summer palace there.

67
00:06:33.336 --> 00:06:39.321
He planned for it to be bigger and better than the Arab palaces, and built in a Christian

68
00:06:39.381 --> 00:06:47.408
style. But unfortunately for Charles V, the construction wasn't completed until 1957 because he ran out of money.

69
00:06:47.428 --> 00:06:59.885
Oops! Today the Alhambra is the only remaining palace complex that can show us the beauty of medieval

70
00:06:59.965 --> 00:07:01.866
Muslim design and architecture.

71
00:07:02.967 --> 00:07:08.191
Sadly it no longer looks exactly like it did during the Middle Ages and the Renaissance.

72
00:07:09.372 --> 00:07:18.659
After 1492 Christian monarchs regained control of Spain and destroyed sections of the Alhambra, rebuilding them in European

73
00:07:18.779 --> 00:07:19.880
Renaissance style.

74
00:07:20.948 --> 00:07:28.813
The Alhambra has since faced fires, earthquakes, gunpowder explosions, and wreckage caused by tourists.

75
00:07:30.053 --> 00:07:37.518
As recently as fifty years ago, visitors were carving their names into the walls and chipping off pieces

76
00:07:37.578 --> 00:07:39.239
of tiles as souvenirs!

77
00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:48.344
Luckily in 1984 the Alhambra was named a World Heritage site, so it's now strictly preserved.

78
00:07:50.024 --> 00:07:52.826
Well, I'd better keep practicing my Spanish.

79
00:07:53.727 --> 00:07:59.491
My parents said I can only go to Spain if I speak nothing but Spanish to them until

80
00:07:59.631 --> 00:08:05.314
June. And a trip filled with medieval castles sounds too fun to pass up!

81
00:08:06.395 --> 00:08:07.316
See you next time!
